  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA shift in the overall weather pattern starts to take shape \nSunday into Monday across the region as a more moist southerly \nflow becomes established. It will be a humid day Sunday with \nlimited sunshine as scattered showers and isolated thunderstorms\ndevelop over western portions of the area during the morning \nand gradually spread eastward and increase in coverage ahead of \na slow-moving frontal boundary, which is expected to have a \nsurface low riding along it, and a supporting upper level \ntrough. Meanwhile a strong Bermuda high will start to slowly \nshift west and between these two features, a strong moisture \ntransport develops with mainly uni-directional shear vectors and\nwarm cloud layer depths increase to nearly 12,000 feet. \n\nAs PWATs increase Sunday afternoon into Sunday night to around \n1.70- 2.00 inches, locally heavy rainfall is possible late \nSunday into Monday, and with winds from just above the surface \nthrough 500mb lining up pretty well, there is the concern for \ntraining to develop. This potential is also highlighted by MBE \nVelocity vectors on model soundings under 10 knots, especially \nbetween 2 AM and 7 AM Monday. It's during this window in \nparticular that the threat for localized flash flooding seems \nhighest, but if the frontal boundary continues to trend slower, \nthis threat could last longer into Monday. With there being some\nuncertainty on timing and location of the heaviest rain in this\nconvective set up, a blend of the NBM and WPC was used to \nremove any convective bullseyes but still highlight the area of\ngreatest concern over the southern Catskills and the upper \nDelaware River basin given recent heavy rains. The risk for \nthunder does look low Sunday/Sunday night with little in the way\nof instability, although shear does start to increase during \nthe early morning hours Monday. For now, thunder was capped at a\nlow-end chance in the forecast. There is a little bit of an \nincrease in instability as Monday rolls around with MLCAPE \nvalues increasing to around 500 J/kg and MUCAPE increasing to \naround or a bit higher than 1000 J/kg, especially east of I-81. \n\nThe slow-moving cold front looks to cross the area Monday \nafternoon and evening as winds shift to the northwest and drier \nair starts to funnel in later in the day and into Monday night. \nHigh pressure then builds in for a brief reprieve from the wet \nweather for Tuesday and possibly into portions of Wednesday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \n\nSeveral features all look to come together to keep the chance \nfor showers and thunderstorms from later Wednesday through late \nweek. The Bermuda high is expected to shift even more west, \ndrawing up plenty of very warm and humid air from the south and \ndriving up dew points. Meanwhile a broad upper trough over the \nnorthern Plains, Upper Midwest and Great Lakes will channel \nwaves of energy toward our region and a weakening cut off upper \nlow over the Tennessee Valley looks to gradually drift northeast\nacross the southern and central Appalachians later in the week.\nThe next frontal boundary also looks to try to approach from \nthe west in the Thursday-Friday time frame and perhaps stall a \nbit. So putting everything together, there will be daily chances\nfor showers and storms with dew points anywhere from the mid \n60s to as high as the mid 70s. This will lead to some very \nuncomfortable conditions with high temperatures mainly in the \nupper 70s to mid 80s each afternoon.",
